      <h1>Mathematically Proven DSP &amp; Hardware Synth Factory</h1>
      <p>
        Perception treats audio synthesis as an exact engineering discipline: unlimited routing in
        software, machine-checked DSP proofs under the hood, and a direct path from desktop patch
        to standalone hardware.
      </p>

      <div class="prose-callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Zero hidden processing:</strong> Every gain, filter, EQ, compressor, send, and
          limiter is a visible node. No secret clipper or auto-gain ever colours your patch.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Machine-checked synthesis proofs</h2>
      <p>
        Core DSP formulas are formally verified in Lean with zero axioms and replayed in CI.
        Mutating a formula breaks its proof immediately:
      </p>
      <div class="prose-table-wrap">
        <table class="prose-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Theorem</th>
              <th>Guarantee</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>travelingWavePropagation</code></td><td>Waveguide delay-line energy and propagation stay exact.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>allpassMagnitude</code></td><td>Allpass dispersion preserves unit magnitude across frequencies.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>weightedOverlapAdd</code></td><td>Spectral STFT frames reconstruct cleanly without window ripple.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>antiderivativeAverage</code></td><td>Nonlinear waveshapers suppress aliasing via ADAA bounds.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>radixTwoSplit</code></td><td>FFT butterfly decomposition matches exact DFT bins.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>macroCurveMonotone</code></td><td>Hardware and UI macro exponents (0.25–4.0) stay strictly in range.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Hardware synth factory</h2>
      <p>
        Perception is the factory; hardware boxes are typed <strong>device profiles</strong>.
        A 4-knob pedal, a 16-knob desktop synth, or the reference <strong>Raspberry Pi 5 + Perception HAT</strong>
        enclosure all run the exact same DSP engine:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Profile-driven faces:</strong> Perception reads a box's physical controls over the link and binds your patch macros directly to its encoders and switches.</li>
        <li><strong>Real-time Linux audio:</strong> Dedicated isolated CPU core, <code class="mono">SCHED_FIFO</code> audio thread, locked memory, direct ALSA I/O at 64-frame periods (&lt; 10 ms bound), and a hardware watchdog with relay bypass.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Standalone DAW &amp; VST3 plug-in</h2>
      <p>
        Run Perception standalone for full arrangement and bus routing, or drop it into Ableton,
        Reaper, Bitwig, Cubase, or Logic as a <strong>VST3 plug-in</strong>. Inside any patch,
        third-party VST3 instruments and effects run in isolated worker processes so a crashing
        plug-in never takes down your session.
      </p>

      <h2>One human-readable JSON patch &amp; song file</h2>
      <p>
        Every note, module, cable, parallel outlet send, stereo bus, and layout coordinate lives
        in one schema-validated JSON file:
      </p>
      <pre class="prose-code"><code>{
  "sendId": 1,
  "source": 3,
  "outlet": 0,
  "bus": "undertow",
  "level": 0.19,
  "enabled": true
}</code></pre>
